Welcome to notebook fourteen. Two threads have been running side by side through this whole track. Expected failure, the kinds you have modelled as Option, Try, and Either in notebook nine. And unexpected failure, the kinds the java virtual machine raises as throwables when something genuinely goes wrong. A network drop. A corrupted file. A bug. This notebook draws a line between them, then adds the missing piece. How to clean up resources, open files, sockets, database connections, in the presence of either kind of failure.

Three sets of tools to learn. First, throw, try, catch, and finally. The java virtual machine mechanism that sits underneath everything else. Scala inherits Java's exception model essentially unchanged. Second, scala dot util dot Using. The standard library equivalent of Java's try with resources. It guarantees cleanup and lifts the result into a Try. Third, patterns for async. What changes once Future is in the picture, and where you need to reach for I O or Z I O instead. By the end you should know, for any given may fail operation, should I model it with Either, wrap it in Try, let the exception propagate, or move to an effect system.

Every java virtual machine language inherits a single error signalling primitive. A thrown throwable unwinds the stack until a matching catch handles it, or it reaches the top and crashes the thread. Scala uses exactly that mechanism. The throw keyword is an expression of type Nothing. It never returns a value, so the compiler is happy to let it stand in for any expected type. That is why you can write, if condition then throw, else value, and the whole thing still type checks as the type of value.

A small example. Define divide taking two integers. If b equals zero, throw a new illegal argument exception saying b must be non zero. Otherwise return a divided by b. Now wrap a call to divide ten and zero in try, with a catch branch matching case e of type illegal argument exception giving back minus one. The result variable is minus one.

Notice that try catch in Scala is itself an expression. The try block and each catch branch produce a value, and the whole construct returns one of them. That is why you can write val r equals try, catch. The catch branches are pattern matches, exactly like a match. Specific exception types, name bindings, and guards all behave the same way you learned in notebook eight.

Now to a subtle but important rule. Catch only what is safe. A bare case underscore of type throwable is almost always wrong. It will swallow out of memory error, stack overflow error, and interrupted exception. Errors that mean the java virtual machine or your thread is genuinely in trouble and should propagate. The convention is to use scala dot util dot control dot non fatal as the pattern. It filters out the fatal cases for you.

So you write a function safeRun taking a body parameter by name. The function returns Either of throwable and A. The implementation is try Right of body, catch case non fatal of e arrow Left of e. Call it with ten divided by two and you get Right of five. Call it with ten divided by zero and you get Left of an arithmetic exception. Call it with throw new out of memory error, and non fatal does not catch it, so the error propagates up.

Note the body colon double arrow A parameter. That is a by name parameter, so the expression is not evaluated until safeRun calls it inside the try. Without by name, the exception would fire on the caller's line, never reaching the try. This is the same trick that Try uses internally. In fact, Try is essentially safeRun with a richer API. Prefer Try for one off captures. Reach for raw try catch when you need multiple catch clauses, want to return shapes other than Try, or are interoperating with code that throws specific exception types you want to handle differently.

Now to resource cleanup. A finally block runs whether the try succeeded, threw, or even if a catch branch itself threw. Use it to release resources. Close a file. Return a connection to the pool. Anything that must be released regardless of outcome. The classic pattern looks like this. Open a buffered reader wrapping a file reader on a path. Then try read line, finally reader dot close. The reader is opened before the try, so a failure during construction does not slip past the cleanup, and it is closed in finally, so a read line failure still releases the file descriptor.

Three reasons not to write this by hand though. First, an exception in finally wins. If read line throws and then close also throws, only the second exception escapes. The original cause is silently lost. This is a real source of misleading stack traces in production. Second, one resource per nesting level. Two resources mean two try finally pairs, each with its own setup and teardown. The boilerplate compounds quickly. Third, it is easy to forget. Any future edit can introduce an early return or a new throw between open and close. The cleanup is not enforced by the compiler. The fix is Using.

The Using object's apply method takes a resource and a body that uses it. It guarantees the resource is closed when the body finishes, success or failure, and returns a Try of A carrying the body's result, or any exception, with a suppressed close exception attached if one also occurred. It is the Scala idiomatic equivalent of Java's try with resources.

So the first line example becomes, def first line of path, returning a Try of String, equals Using of a new buffered reader on a new file reader on the path, followed by a body that calls reader dot read line. Pass it a real file path and you get Success of the string. Pass it a missing path and you get Failure of a file not found exception.

Two things to notice. First, the body's result is wrapped in Try. The same shape from notebook nine, so all of map, flat map, recover, and the for comprehension work on it directly. Second, you did not write close anywhere. Using looks up a typeclass instance that knows how to release the resource and calls it for you. The typeclass is named Using dot Releasable of R. The standard library already supplies an instance for anything that extends Java's auto closeable interface, which includes most java input output types, so you usually get it for free.

What if your resource does not already implement auto closeable. You have two choices. Make it implement auto closeable, or provide a Releasable given. Both are short. For the first option, define a class Session extending auto closeable, with a name field, a query method, and a close method that prints closed and the name. Call Using on a new Session of d b, with a body that calls s dot query select one. The output shows opened d b on construction, the body runs, and then closed d b prints automatically. The result is Success of the query string.

For the second option, define a class Cache with a name field, a get method, and a shutdown method. Cache does not extend auto closeable. So you define a given Using dot Releasable of Cache with, def release taking a cache c equals c dot shutdown. Now Using on a Cache works, and shutdown is called on the way out. The first form, extending auto closeable, is the right default. The Releasable given is what you reach for when you cannot modify the resource type. Typically a third party class that does not extend auto closeable but does have some other stop or shutdown method.

Real code often opens several resources at once. A database connection plus a result set reader. An H T T P client plus a response stream. Nesting Using dot apply calls works for two resources and stops being readable at three. Using dot Manager flattens the nesting. You register each resource with the manager as you acquire it, and the manager releases them all in reverse order when the block ends.

So copy first line looks like this. Using dot Manager taking a block where use is the manager handle. Val input equals use of a new buffered reader on a new file reader on the source path. Val output equals use of a new print writer on a new file writer on the destination path. Then output dot println of input dot read line. Each use call registers the resource and returns it. When the block exits, for any reason, the manager calls close on each in last in first out order. If both a body exception and a close exception occur, the body exception wins and the close exception is attached as suppressed, so you do not lose information the way you do with hand written try finally.

Now bridge the two error worlds. Using returns a Try, which means failure is a throwable. Often you would rather expose named errors to callers, as in notebook nine. The translation is one to either conversion followed by left dot map to convert the throwable into your domain error. Define an enum LoadError with two cases. NotFound taking a path. And Unreadable taking a path and a cause throwable. Then def load first line of path returning Either of LoadError and String. The body is Using on a buffered reader on a file reader, followed by a body that reads one line. Then dot to either. Then dot left dot map with two cases. Case underscore of type file not found exception arrow LoadError dot NotFound of path. Case other arrow LoadError dot Unreadable of path and other.

Read that as the bridge between the two error worlds. Inside, a Try from Using. Capture shaped, throwable typed. Outside, an Either of LoadError and String. Domain shaped, enum typed. Every caller now has to handle NotFound and Unreadable explicitly, and adding a third case to LoadError would force the compiler to flag every match downstream.

Now to async. Using is synchronous. The block must produce its result before the resource is closed, which means if your block returns a Future of A, the resource is closed before the future completes, and the asynchronous read will fail or return garbage. Two patterns avoid the trap. Pattern one. Keep the input output inside the Future block, so the resource lives and dies on one async thread. Pattern two. Use Future's and then combinator, or transform, to register cleanup that runs when the future completes.

In pattern one, async first line of path returns Future of String equals Future of, Using of a buffered reader on a file reader, body that reads one line, then dot get. In pattern two, async line count of path opens a buffered reader before the Future, then runs Future of iterator continually of read line, take while not null, dot size. Then chains dot and then with case underscore arrow reader dot close. Pattern one is the right default. Short lived resource. Contained lifetime. Pattern two is what you reach for when the resource is shared across multiple async steps. The and then callback runs on completion regardless of success or failure.

Neither is perfect. There is no built in equivalent of Using dot Manager for Future. If you find yourself stacking these patterns, that is the signal to move to the cats effect library's Resource of I O and A, or to Z I O's Scope type. Both give you composable, leak safe resource management with the same for comprehension feel as everything else in notebooks nine and thirteen.

Now to a question that surfaces every time. Throw versus return. You now have five ways to surface a failure. The choice is rarely arbitrary. Return Option of A when absence is the only failure mode and no reason is needed. Return Try of A when wrapping a call that throws and the caller just wants value or exception. Return Either of E and A when failures are part of your domain and the caller must distinguish them. Throw an exception when the failure is a bug. A precondition violation. A should never happen. Or when you are at a boundary that uses exceptions, like a java callback, a servlet, or an actor's receive method. And let it propagate when you cannot do anything useful with the failure at this layer. Do not write empty catch blocks. Let a boundary handler deal with it.

The bias should be toward returning values, not throwing. Throwing makes a failure invisible in the type signature. A function that returns Either of E and A cannot be misused. The compiler forces the caller to address E. A function that throws can be misused on every call site.

A short list of the pitfalls everyone meets at least once. First, catching throwable. Use non fatal, or specific exception types. A bare throwable swallows java virtual machine level errors that need to crash the process. Second, catching interrupted exception and continuing. When the java virtual machine interrupts a thread, it is asking the thread to stop. Either let it propagate, or call thread dot current thread dot interrupt after catching it, so the signal is not lost. Third, returning null from a catch. Turns one failure into a downstream null pointer exception. Return Option, Try, or Either instead. Fourth, forgetting close in try finally. Use Using. Guaranteed cleanup is the entire reason it exists. Fifth, closing a resource that is about to be used asynchronously. The resource will be gone by the time the async work touches it. Either keep the lifetime inside the Future block, or move to Resource or Scope. Sixth, using Try for domain errors. If you keep pattern matching on Failure of an underscore of type my exception, that is an Either of E and A trying to escape. Promote the failure to a named algebraic data type.

Let us close with a worked example that ties the pieces together. A small config loader. Read a file. Parse each line as key equals value. Return either a typed config map or a typed error. Define enum ConfigError with three cases. NotFound taking a path. Unreadable taking a path and a cause. And BadLine taking a path and a line. Then def load config of path returning Either of ConfigError and Map of String and String.

The body has two steps. Step one, read raw lines. Use Using on a buffered reader on a file reader, with a body that pulls an iterator of read line calls, takes while not null, trims each line, filters out blanks and lines starting with a hash, and collects to a list. Then dot to either. Then dot left dot map with two cases. File not found exception arrow ConfigError dot NotFound of path. Other arrow ConfigError dot Unreadable of path and other.

Step two, parse the lines. Flat map on the right side, taking the list of lines, and fold left starting with an empty map wrapped as Right. On each step, if the accumulator is Right, split the line on equals with limit two. If you get an array of two elements named k and v, update the accumulator with k trimmed and v trimmed. Otherwise return Left of ConfigError dot BadLine. If the accumulator is already Left, pass it through unchanged.

Walk through what just happened. Using opens the file and guarantees the close. The body collects raw lines, dropping blanks and comments. To either and left dot map translate the input output throwable into a domain error. NotFound for missing files. Unreadable for everything else. Then flat map runs the parsing step, which can fail per line with BadLine. The fold short circuits on the first Left and otherwise accumulates entries into the map.

The whole pipeline produces an Either of ConfigError and Map of String and String. Typed input. Typed failures. No exception escape from inside. This is the shape to aim for at the edges of every Scala program. Errors named, resources released, the type signature telling the truth about what can go wrong.

Notebook fifteen returns to the motivating goal of the whole track. Scala for Spark. You will see how the constructs you have learned, case classes, pattern matching, the collection combinators, Option and Either, futures, and the resource and error patterns from this notebook, combine to read Spark code idiomatically and write it without surprises. The Scala foundation ends here. The data engineering payoff starts there.
